# PR guardrails

This checkout is the personal fork `1123/kafka`. Ship work on a feature branch of that fork. Do not push to `apache/kafka`, do not open a pull request against upstream, and do not commit on `trunk`.

A KIP is required before changing a public interface, the wire protocol, a configuration, or a metric. The public client surface is the `javadoc { include ... }` list on `:clients` in `build.gradle` (`org.apache.kafka.clients.admin`, `consumer`, `producer`, and the `org.apache.kafka.common` packages listed there). Other modules publish their own javadoc includes (Connect API, Streams, coordinator `api` packages). If the request does not include an accepted KIP, stop at the boundary and say which packages would have to change.

## Leave these alone unless the user asked for that file

- Generated sources: `**/generated/**`, `**/generated-test/**`. Edit the JSON message specs and run `./gradlew processMessages processTestMessages`.
- Release and build infrastructure: `release/`, `gradle/wrapper/`, `wrapper.gradle`, `gradle/dependencies.gradle` version pins, Docker promotion and official-image workflows, `committer-tools/`.
- Checkstyle, SpotBugs, RAT, Spotless, and `org.apache.kafka.public-api-checker` configuration. Fix the code instead of disabling a check.
- `checkstyle/import-control*.xml`, unless the task is explicitly an import-control change. Clients must not take a dependency on the broker.

## Compatibility

Source compatibility follows `modulesNeedingJava11` in `build.gradle`. Clients, generator, streams, and `test-common:test-common-util` stay on Java 11. Every other module stays on Java 17. Do not bump a protocol version's lower bound; dropping an old message version requires a KIP (`clients/src/main/resources/common/message/README.md`).

## Every code change

- New source files include the Apache license header. Java must match `checkstyle/java.header`. RAT excludes are in the root `build.gradle` `rat` block; `.cursor/**` is excluded because Cursor rules and skills have to start with YAML frontmatter. `template.java` still has the header.
- AI-assisted commits carry exactly one AI trailer, `Generated-by: <tool name and version>` (for example `Generated-by: Grok 4.7`), as in `CONTRIBUTING.md`. Do not add `Co-Authored-By` for a tool: Git expects `Name <email>` there.
- Commits are authored by the human developer who runs the agent, never by a bot identity such as "Cursor Agent". Do not hard-code anyone's name or email.
  - Locally, use the developer's own `git config user.name` / `user.email` as they are. If they are unset, stop and ask before committing.
  - In a cloud agent, take the identity from the task's `Author` field (`Name <email>`) and run `git config user.name "<name>"` and `git config user.email "<email>"` before the first commit. If the task gives no author, commit anyway and say in the summary that the developer must run `git commit --amend --reset-author` before opening a pull request.
  - Check with `git log -1 --format='%an <%ae>'`.
- Commit subjects use `KAFKA-XXXXX` only when a real JIRA exists; otherwise `MINOR` or `HOTFIX`.
- Secrets stay in the environment (`CURSOR_API_KEY`, `SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET`). Do not write them into source, workflows, or logs.

## Upgrade notes

A change that operators or client users can notice (protocol version bump, new or changed config, metric, CLI option or output, changed default or behavior) gets one bullet in `docs/getting-started/upgrade.md`.

- The target release is `version=` in `gradle.properties` without `-SNAPSHOT` (for example `4.5.0-SNAPSHOT` means 4.5.0). Put the bullet under `## Upgrading to <that version>` / `### Notable changes in <that version>`, never under an older, already released section.
- If that section does not exist yet, add it at the top of the file in the same format as the existing ones.
- Say what changed, what users see, what stays compatible, and link the KIP or JIRA.

## Upstream-ready branch

Work happens on a branch of this fork that is based on `cursor-scaffolding`, so it contains the Cursor and Grok Bot scaffolding. That scaffolding must never reach `apache/kafka`. When the implementation is done, also prepare a clean branch the user can open an upstream pull request from himself:

1. Get current upstream trunk: `git fetch upstream trunk`, or in a cloud agent without that remote `git fetch https://github.com/apache/kafka trunk` and use `FETCH_HEAD`.
2. Create `<JIRA-ID>-upstream` (for example `KAFKA-15923-upstream`) from that trunk and cherry-pick only the feature commits. Never cherry-pick scaffolding, task, or plan commits.
3. Verify that `git diff --name-only <trunk>...HEAD` lists none of `.cursor/`, `grok-bot/`, `scripts/cursor_api_trigger.py`, `scripts/simulate_ci.sh`, `.github/workflows/agent-guardrails.yml`, the README setup section, or the `.cursor/**` RAT exclude in `build.gradle`.
4. Re-run the targeted tests and Checkstyle on that branch.
5. Push it to `origin` (`1123/kafka`) only. The user opens the upstream pull request himself.

